  "documentationOpenTopicAction": "Open topic",
  "documentationQuickAnswersTitle": "Quick answers for the most common doubts",
  "documentationQuickAnswersBody": "Before opening every card, start with these short answers. If one already solves your question, you save time.",
  "documentationAnswerWorkQuestion": "Can I work with visitor status alone?",
  "documentationAnswerWorkAnswer": "No. Formal work requires a compatible migration status and regular registration.",
  "documentationAnswerCpfQuestion": "Does CPF alone solve banking and contracts?",
  "documentationAnswerCpfAnswer": "No. CPF helps a lot, but it usually does not replace a regular migration document.",
  "documentationAnswerRegistrationQuestion": "Is migration registration completed immediately?",
  "documentationAnswerRegistrationAnswer": "No. The protocol already matters while the CRNM is being produced, so the process does not depend on an instant card.",
  "documentationAnswerStayQuestion": "Is staying longer as a visitor the same as living regularly?",
  "documentationAnswerStayAnswer": "No. For someone who plans to live in Brazil, regular residence is usually the right path.",
  "documentationAnswerSusQuestion": "Can a foreign national use SUS?",
  "documentationAnswerSusAnswer": "Yes. SUS is universal in Brazil, and the Ministry of Health explicitly reaffirms access for foreign nationals.",
  "documentationAnswerSusCardQuestion": "Do I need a SUS card or CPF before getting care?",
  "documentationAnswerSusCardAnswer": "Not necessarily. Registration helps follow-up care, but initial access and especially urgent care should not depend on having everything ready.",
  "documentationAnswerForeignLicenseQuestion": "Can I drive at first with my foreign license?",
  "documentationAnswerForeignLicenseAnswer": "In general, yes for a limited period, with a valid document and subject to the applicable agreement. After that, it is better to confirm with the state Detran.",
  "documentationAnswerBrazilianLicenseQuestion": "Can I later get a Brazilian license?",
  "documentationAnswerBrazilianLicenseAnswer": "Yes, if you are regular in the country and meet Detran requirements. The process and fees vary by state.",
  "documentationAnswerWorkCardQuestion": "Does formal employment still exist and how does it work?",
  "documentationAnswerWorkCardAnswer": "Yes. In formal CLT employment, the relationship is registered and the Carteira de Trabalho Digital holds the work record.",
  "documentationAnswerPjQuestion": "Is PJ work the same as monotributo?",
  "documentationAnswerPjAnswer": "It may feel similar as a self-employed or company-based model, but it is not the same legal structure. Tax, retirement, and contract rules vary by arrangement in Brazil.",
  "documentationAnswerInssQuestion": "Is public retirement in Brazil the INSS?",
  "documentationAnswerInssAnswer": "Yes. INSS is the main public retirement gateway for benefits such as retirement, as long as contributions and eligibility rules are met.",
  "documentationAnswerRetirementQuestion": "Does retirement depend only on age?",
  "documentationAnswerRetirementAnswer": "No. Minimum age matters, but contribution time and transition rules matter too.",
  "documentationHealthSectionTitle": "Public health vs private health",
  "documentationHealthSectionBody": "The key is understanding the role of each path. Public health is not a cheap insurance plan, and private health does not replace careful coverage comparison.",
  "documentationWorkSectionTitle": "How work and retirement connect",
  "documentationWorkSectionBody": "It helps to separate your work model from the way you contribute. Formal employment, CNPJ-based work, and INSS contribution are related, but not the same thing.",
  "documentationDrivingSectionTitle": "How to think about driving without overcomplicating it",
  "documentationDrivingSectionBody": "The safest flow is to split this into three questions: can I drive now, what must I validate in the state, and when is it worth starting the Brazilian license process.",
  "documentationDeepDiveTitle": "If you need one more level of detail",
  "documentationDeepDiveBody": "This is where the full cards with official sources stay. They are still short, but they help when the quick answer is not enough.",
  "documentationCostsTitle": "Approximate costs that help with first decisions",
  "documentationCostsBody": "When a national value or useful official reference exists, the app shows an approximate conversion to support your first reading.",
