  "documentationCostsUnavailable": "The exchange rate could not be updated right now. Values in BRL remain as the reference.",
  "documentationCostsDisclaimer": "Use this as early guidance only. Costs vary by state, provider, age, coverage, and local rules.",
  "documentationCostFreeValue": "Free",
  "documentationCostVariableValue": "Variable",
  "documentationCostCpfTitle": "Official CPF request",
  "documentationCostCpfSupporting": "The official request is free; the app treats it as zero cost.",
  "documentationCostSusCardTitle": "SUS card and first registration",
  "documentationCostSusCardSupporting": "Public registration and issuance do not usually require direct payment.",
  "documentationCostPublicCareTitle": "Initial SUS care",
  "documentationCostPublicCareSupporting": "A UBS, health post, or public entry point does not work like a paid private consultation.",
  "documentationCostDrivingTitle": "First driving license",
  "documentationCostDrivingValue": "Official example",
  "documentationCostDrivingSupporting": "Recent Detran-ES reference: R$ 533.34. Your state and driving school may charge differently.",
  "documentationCostPrivateHealthTitle": "Private health plan",
  "documentationCostPrivateHealthSupporting": "There is no single national price. Age, coverage, network, and waiting periods can change the final cost a lot.",
  "documentationCpfTitle": "CPF",
  "documentationCpfSummary": "The first practical document that helps with banking, contracts, and registrations.",
  "documentationCpfBulletOne": "A foreign national can request a CPF; in Brazil, the process can be started online or through a partnered entity.",
  "documentationCpfBulletTwo": "The official service lists an estimated time of up to 30 calendar days.",
  "documentationCpfBulletThree": "CPF does not replace your migration document, but it usually unlocks a large part of daily life.",
  "documentationRegistrationTitle": "Migration registration and CRNM",
  "documentationRegistrationSummary": "After entering regularly, registration with the Federal Police is usually the key next step.",
  "documentationRegistrationBulletOne": "Anyone who enters with a temporary visa must register within 90 days after entering Brazil.",
  "documentationRegistrationBulletTwo": "If residence authorization was granted inside Brazil, registration must happen within 30 days.",
  "documentationRegistrationBulletThree": "The CRNM may take around 30 business days to be produced; the official service allows a longer total window and the protocol preserves rights.",
  "documentationStayTitle": "How long can I stay",
  "documentationStaySummary": "For an Argentinian user, the practical path is usually regular residence instead of relying on visitor stay.",
  "documentationStayBulletOne": "A visitor visa is not designed for living in Brazil or for paid work.",
  "documentationStayBulletTwo": "Residence under the Mercosur Agreement can be granted for 2 years.",
  "documentationStayBulletThree": "Before that period ends, you may request conversion to indefinite residence if you meet the requirements.",
  "documentationWorkBankTitle": "Work and bank account",
  "documentationWorkBankSummary": "Working and opening an account depend more on regular status than on a single magic document.",
  "documentationWorkBankBulletOne": "A visitor visa does not authorize paid work in Brazil.",
  "documentationWorkBankBulletTwo": "To work formally, you need a compatible migration status and regular registration.",
  "documentationWorkBankBulletThree": "A bank may request additional documents; CPF helps, but a regular migration document usually matters in onboarding.",
  "documentationCitizenshipTitle": "Naturalization",
  "documentationCitizenshipSummary": "Brazilian nationality does not come from CPF time alone; it depends on regular residence and its own legal rules.",
  "documentationCitizenshipBulletOne": "Ordinary naturalization generally requires indefinite residence in Brazil.",
  "documentationCitizenshipBulletTwo": "The general rule requires 4 years of residence before applying, along with other legal requirements.",
  "documentationCitizenshipBulletThree": "There are official cases that reduce that period, so it is worth checking the exact rule before planning your path.",
  "documentationHealthPublicTitle": "SUS, local health posts, and public access",
  "documentationHealthPublicSummary": "Public health in Brazil is not a prepaid entry plan. The logic is universal access, with different entry points depending on what you need.",
  "documentationHealthPublicBulletOne": "SUS provides universal access, including for foreign nationals in Brazil.",
  "documentationHealthPublicBulletTwo": "A UBS or local health post is usually the first door for routine care, follow-up, vaccines, and basic care.",
  "documentationHealthPublicBulletThree": "Urgent and emergency care follow a different access path; do not wait until every registration step is finished before seeking help.",
  "documentationHealthFlowTitle": "How to find the right kind of care",
  "documentationHealthFlowSummary": "Not every health question starts at a hospital. It helps to know when to look for a UBS, UPA, hospital, or official app.",
  "documentationHealthFlowBulletOne": "Use a UBS or health post for routine care, referrals, prescriptions, and follow-up.",
  "documentationHealthFlowBulletTwo": "Use a UPA or hospital when the case is urgent, acute, or cannot wait for a basic appointment.",
  "documentationHealthFlowBulletThree": "Meu SUS Digital and the local health department help locate units, exams, and follow-up information.",
  "documentationHealthPrivateTitle": "Private health",
  "documentationHealthPrivateSummary": "A private plan may improve convenience and network speed, but it becomes a recurring cost and needs careful coverage comparison.",
  "documentationHealthPrivateBulletOne": "Private health plans are paid products regulated by ANS.",
  "documentationHealthPrivateBulletTwo": "Price, network, coverage, and waiting periods vary by contract, age, and operator.",
  "documentationHealthPrivateBulletThree": "Before signing, compare network, coverage, and rules in official ANS material, not only the price.",
  "documentationWorkCltTitle": "Formal employment",
  "documentationWorkCltSummary": "In formal work, the employment relationship follows CLT and the record appears in the Carteira de Trabalho Digital.",
  "documentationWorkCltBulletOne": "Formal employment is the clearest and most recognizable model of formal work in Brazil.",
  "documentationWorkCltBulletTwo": "Your work history can be followed through the Carteira de Trabalho Digital.",
  "documentationWorkCltBulletThree": "In this model, the link with retirement contributions is usually more integrated into payroll.",
  "documentationWorkPjTitle": "PJ, CNPJ, and independent work",
  "documentationWorkPjSummary": "Working as a PJ or through a CNPJ changes the logic of the relationship. It may resemble monotributo culturally, but it is not the same legal structure.",
  "documentationWorkPjBulletOne": "PJ is not formal employment; the relationship is business-based or self-employed, not employment-based.",
  "documentationWorkPjBulletTwo": "Opening a CNPJ and contributing to retirement are connected topics, but not automatic in every case.",
  "documentationWorkPjBulletThree": "Before accepting this model, understand taxes, contract terms, and how retirement contributions will work.",
  "documentationRetirementTitle": "Public retirement and pension system",
  "documentationRetirementSummary": "In Brazil, public retirement revolves around INSS, with minimum age, contribution time, and transition rules shaping each case differently.",
  "documentationRetirementBulletOne": "The current general age-based rule uses a minimum age of 62 for women and 65 for men.",
  "documentationRetirementBulletTwo": "Contribution time still matters, especially under transition rules and eligibility analysis.",
  "documentationRetirementBulletThree": "For someone arriving from abroad, it is safest to understand early how contributions will happen in Brazil.",
  "documentationDrivingTitle": "First driving license in Brazil",
  "documentationDrivingSummary": "If you are going to live in Brazil, a Brazilian license depends on the state Detran and a local process with mandatory steps.",
  "documentationDrivingBulletOne": "The process usually includes medical and psychological exams, classes, a theory test, and a practical test.",
  "documentationDrivingBulletTwo": "A regularized foreign national can enter the process if the state identification and residence requirements are met.",
  "documentationDrivingBulletThree": "Fees and final cost vary by Detran and driving school, so treat the displayed value only as orientation.",
  "documentationForeignLicenseTitle": "Foreign license and initial driving",
  "documentationForeignLicenseSummary": "Having a valid foreign license may help at the beginning, but it does not permanently replace the need to confirm the Brazilian rule.",
  "documentationForeignLicenseBulletOne": "The ability to drive with a foreign license depends on validity, identification, and the rule that applies to your case.",
  "documentationForeignLicenseBulletTwo": "An initial period of use does not mean automatic equivalence for your entire stay in Brazil.",
  "documentationForeignLicenseBulletThree": "If you plan to settle in Brazil, confirm early with the state Detran whether there will be registration, exchange, or a full new process.",
